  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef is sick of solving problems relating to binary representations of integers, so he decides to change things up — obviously, 333 is larger than 222, so ternary must be better than binary.\n\nChef thinks an integer is good if its ternary (that is, base 333) representation contains the digit 222.\n\nFor example, 5,6,5, 6, 5,6, and 141414 are good (their ternary representations are 12(3),20(3),112(3)\\texttt{12}_{(3)}, \\texttt{20}_{(3)}, \\texttt{112}_{(3)}12(3)​,20(3)​,112(3)​ respectively), while 121212 is not (being 110(3)\\texttt{110}_{(3)}110(3)​ in ternary).",
    "constraints": [
      "†^\\dagger† A permutation of the integers {1,2,…,N}\\{1, 2, \\ldots, N\\}{1,2,…,N} is an array of length NNN that contains every integer from 111 to NNN, exactly once.",
      "Input Format",
      "The first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.",
      "The first and only line of each test case will contain a single integer NNN.",
      "Output Format",
      "For each test case, output on a new line NNN space-separated integers P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​.",
      "PPP should be a permutation of {1,2,…,N}\\{1, 2, \\ldots, N\\}{1,2,…,N}, and (Pi+i)(P_i + i)(Pi​+i) should be good for each iii between 111 and NNN.",
      "If there are multiple possible solutions, you may print **any** of them.",
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "3≤N≤2⋅1053 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^53≤N≤2⋅105",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n5\n6",
        "output": "1 3 2\n4 5 2 1 3\n5 4 2 3 6 1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: For P=[1,3,2]P = [1, 3, 2]P=[1,3,2], the sequence of (Pi+i)(P_i + i)(Pi​+i) values is [2,5,5][2, 5, 5][2,5,5].\n222 is 2(3)\\texttt{2}_{(3)}2(3)​ in ternary, while 555 is 12(3)\\texttt{12}_{(3)}12(3)​, so they're both good.\nTest case 222: For P=[4,5,2,1,3]P = [4, 5, 2, 1, 3]P=[4,5,2,1,3], the sequence of (Pi+i)(P_i + i)(Pi​+i) values is [5,7,5,5,8][5, 7, 5, 5, 8][5,7,5,5,8]. It can be verified that all of these are good integers.\nTest case 333: For P=[5,4,2,3,6,1]P = [5, 4, 2, 3, 6, 1]P=[5,4,2,3,6,1], the sequence of (Pi+i)(P_i + i)(Pi​+i) values is [6,6,5,7,11,7][6, 6, 5, 7, 11, 7][6,6,5,7,11,7]. Again, it can be verified that all of these are good (for instance, 111111 is 102(3)\\texttt{102}_{(3)}102(3)​ in ternary)."
      }
    ]
